    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d346">

                <pb facs="0368" n="356" sameAs="frus:frus1940v01/pg356"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">860D.001/24: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                        Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-12-23T15:00:00+03:00">December 23,
                            1940—3 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received December 23—2:40 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1769. My 1740, December 17, 1 p.m.<anchor n="85" xml:id="d346-f1"/> Although the
                    Soviet press has refrained from any direct comment on the election of Ryti as
                    President of Finland, today’s newspapers reproduce what appears to be a full
                    summary of the speech which he is said to have made yesterday including a
                    quotation of the portion concerning the desire of the Finnish people to maintain
                    friendly and good neighborly relations with the Soviet Union as well as with
                    Germany and Sweden. While not conclusive the reproduction of Ryti’s speech would
                    appear to indicate that the Soviet Government is not displeased with the
                    election of Ryti.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Helsinki.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
